"Problem adding Class Node object to array"},{"tags":["c++","opencv","image-processing","rgb","yuv"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":73920787,"is_answered":false,"question_id":73831908,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1664648572,"creation_date":1664648572,"body":"So I finally figured out how to convert the image with the following code:\n\nI had to add the  to specify the incoming encoding first, then convert to using the YUY2 enum.\n","excerpt":"cv_bridge::toCvCopy(imgPtr, &quot;yuv422&quot;)-&gt;image;\n  // Convert the image frame to a BGR format image\n  cvtColor( image, newimage, COLOR_YUV2BGRA_YUY2 );\n\n  // Write the new image to the out path\nimwrite(<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; ","title":"Convert raw YUV422 image to RGB"},{"tags":["python"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":false,"question_id":73913249,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1664611027,"creation_date":1664566178,"body":"I am trying to move selected images from nested subdirectories. I am match sku from an excel file to the image name (which is also the sku number). Any that matches are then moved into a new folder.\nMy challenge when I try to create a dictionary to save my full directory I am being faced with the following error message.\nFile &quot;c:\\printing\\python\\data_clean.py&quot;, line 56\nfullpath_filelist = {file: os.path.join(root,dirs, file}\n^\nTabError: inconsistent use of tabs and spaces in indentation\n\n","excerpt":"filename in filelist:\n    for rowNum in range(2, sheet.max_row+1):\n    #for rowNum in range(2, 3):\n        image = sheet[&#39;H&#39; + str(rowNum)].value  #This control which column to search from\n        <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; = os.path.join(root,Main_Searchterm,Sub_Searchterm,filename)\n\n        if str(image) == str(filename):\n            shutil.move(filename,<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>)\n\nfind_category() &hellip; ","title":"TabError: inconsistent use of tabs and spaces in indentation when adding to a dictionary"},{"tags":["python","python-3.x","replace"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":73888104,"is_answered":false,"question_id":73887919,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1664402922,"creation_date":1664401330,"body":"The behaviour of the code you wrote is working as I would have expected from reading it. Now, as to how to correct it to do what you expected/wanted it to do is a little different. You don&#39;t necessarily need to replace here, instead, you can consider appending what you need, as it seems from the behaviour you are looking for is in fact appending something to the end of the path before the extension.\nWe can try to help the code a bit by making it a little more &quot;generic&quot; by allowing us to simply &quot;append&quot; anything to the end of a string. The steps we can do to achieve this is (for other readers, yes there are more foolproof ways to do this, for now sticking to a simple example) :\n\nsplit the string at  so that you end up with a list containing:\n\n\n\nAppend to the first element what you need to the end of the string so you end up with:\n\n\n\nUse  to re-craft your string, but use :\n\n\n\nYou will finally get:\n\n\nCoding the above, we can have it work as follows:\n\nMore idiomatic approach using Python&#39;s  module is an interesting solution too.\n\n","excerpt":".&quot;)\n&gt;&gt;&gt; img_split\n[&#39;/home/user/Picture/Image-1&#39;, &#39;jpg&#39;]\n&gt;&gt;&gt; img_split[0] = img_split[0] + &quot;_s&quot;\n&gt;&gt;&gt; img_split\n[&#39;/home/user/Picture/Image-1_s&#39;, &#39;jpg&#39;]\n&gt;&gt;&gt; <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> = &quot;.&quot;.join(img_split)\n&gt;&gt;&gt; <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; ","title":"How can I replace a specific text in string python?"},{"tags":["ruby","xpath","xml-parsing","nokogiri"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":2,"is_answered":true,"question_id":15690269,"item_type":"question","score":1,"last_activity_date":1663901174,"creation_date":1364498065,"body":"My code is supposed to &quot;guess&quot; the path(s) that lies before the relevant text nodes in my XML file. Relevant in this case means: text nodes nested within the recurring product/person/something tag, but not text nodes that are used outside of it.\n\nThis code: \n\n\n\nworks for simple XML files, for example:\n\n\n\nBut when it gets more complicated, how should I then approach the challenge? For example with this one:\n\n\n","excerpt":"(node.name)\n      end\n    end\n    <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> = &quot;/&quot;+path.reverse.join(&quot;/&quot;)\n\n\nworks for simple XML files, for example:\n\n&lt;?xml version=&quot;1.0&quot; encoding=&quot;utf-8&quot;? &hellip; title&lt;/title&gt;\n      &lt;brand&gt;Some product brand&lt;/brand&gt;\n    &lt;/item&gt;\n    &lt;item&gt;\n      &lt;title&gt;Some product title&lt;/title&gt;\n      &lt;brand&gt;Some product brand&lt;/brand&gt;\n    &lt;/item&gt;\n  &lt;/channel&gt;\n&lt;/rss&gt;\n\nputs <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; ","title":"Parsing an XML file with Nokogiri to determine the path (Ruby)"},{"tags":["python","python-3.x","string"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":4,"is_answered":true,"question_id":73207617,"item_type":"question","score":2,"last_activity_date":1659449649,"creation_date":1659443545,"body":"Hi I have a string like this which will be dynamic and can be in following combinations.\n\nSomething like these. In any case I just want this string in 2 parts like  path and filename. Path will not consist of file name for example.\nEnd result expected\n\nTill now tried many things included\n\nAnd  but nothing worked till now.\n","excerpt":"result expected\n    &#39;new&#39;\n    &#39;new/mainfolder&#39;\n    &#39;new/mainfolder/subfolder&#39;\n    &#39;new/mainfolder/subfolder/secondsubfolder&#39;\n\nTill now tried many things included\npath = &#39;new/mainfolder/file.csv&#39;\n<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; ","title":"Remove file name and extension from path and just keep path"},{"tags":["python-3.x","django"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":0,"is_answered":false,"question_id":73054230,"item_type":"question","score":2,"last_activity_date":1658330343,"creation_date":1658330343,"body":"When I run a test case\n\nin django webapp I get some errors.\n\nIn this stackoverflow post stackoverflow answer they suggest to set STATIC_ROOT.\nI don&#39;t know how set this property in settings.py.\nThe path where I have static files (css,js,images) is\n/var/www/html/elastic_queries/python/djangosite/giustiziasite/pygiustizia/static/pygiustizia\nWhat can I do, put path directly in STATIC_ROOT variable?\n","excerpt":"django/views/static.py&quot;, line 35, in serve\n    fullpath = Path(safe_join(document_root, path))\n  File &quot;/home/nicola/.local/lib/python3.9/site-packages/django/utils/_os.py&quot;, line 17, in safe_join\n    <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; django/views/static.py&quot;, line 35, in serve\n    fullpath = Path(safe_join(document_root, path))\n  File &quot;/home/nicola/.local/lib/python3.9/site-packages/django/utils/_os.py&quot;, line 17, in safe_join\n    <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; ","title":"using selenium I get some errors: TypeError: expected str, bytes or os.PathLike object, not NoneType"},{"tags":["azure","pyspark","azure-blob-storage","databricks","azure-databricks"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":72822274,"is_answered":false,"question_id":72820914,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1656624872,"creation_date":1656624872,"body":"The way Spark works is it splits a file in part files over executors to process it in distributed fashion parallelly. So you always get a part file in output while writing data whose name you cannot control and only the output folder name you can control\nSo overall the process you are following is what I would recommend that use repartition/coalesce (1) to put it in single file inside folder, get .parquet file from that folder move it using dbutils and delete the folder\nBelow is the code I use to perform same activity\n\nReplace temp_path and final_path/filename.parquet\n","excerpt":"df.coalesce(1).write.csv(&quot;/temp_path/&quot;,&quot;overwrite&quot;)\nz=[ x.name for x in dbutils.fs.ls(&quot;/temp_path/&quot;) if x.name.endswith(&quot;csv&quot;)]\nprint(z)\nif len(z)==1:\n  print(z[0])\n  dbutils.fs.mv(&quot;/temp_path/&quot;+z[0],&quot;/<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; /filename.parquet&#39;)\ndbutils.fs.rm(&quot;/temp_path/&quot;,True)\n\n\nReplace temp_path and <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>/filename.parquet &hellip; ","title":"Writing dataframe to Azure Blob Storage using Databricks generates an empty file"},{"tags":["python","python-imaging-library","rgb","file-handling","grayscale"],"question_score":-1,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":72813297,"is_answered":false,"question_id":72813145,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1656584253,"creation_date":1656580801,"body":"Probably this code would work for you?\nCode:\n\n","excerpt":"os.listdir(dir))):\n    # directory where images are stored\n    dir = &#39;/content/pizza_steak/test/steak&#39;\n\n    # get the file name\n    file_name = os.listdir(dir)[i]\n\n    # creating a final path\n    <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; (<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>) &hellip; ","title":"Python script to convert RBG image dataset into Grayscale images using pillow"},{"tags":["python","django","matplotlib","django-templates"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":72700848,"is_answered":false,"question_id":44461154,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1655814769,"creation_date":1655814769,"body":"As I had the same problem and the provided answer by @Brobin was a little bit confusing to me I&#39;ll try to clarify it a little bit more.\nConsidering you have the variable:\n\nand you want to use it inside the function:\n\nyou can directly input the variable inside the  function without the  as follows:\n\nNow lets assume you want more complex processing like having an &#39;item&#39; from the &#39;my_path&#39; as if it was a list of paths and also include some other text to that as a path or file type, you&#39;ll have to use the  function:\n\nHope that helps.\n","excerpt":"complex processing like having an &#39;item&#39; from the &#39;my_path&#39; as if it was a list of paths and also include some other text to that as a path or file type, you&#39;ll have to use the with function:\n{% with <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; =&#39;MainSite/videos/My_Sample_Video_&#39;|add:my_path.0|add:&#39;.mp4&#39; %}\n    &lt;source src=&quot;{% static <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> %}&quot; type=&quot;video/mp4&quot;&gt;\n{% endwith %}\n\nHope that helps. &hellip; ","title":"How to use a variable in a Django template between {% %}"},{"tags":["python","python-3.x","unit-testing","python-packaging"],"question_score":8,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":72465979,"is_answered":false,"question_id":56123724,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1654105086,"creation_date":1654105086,"body":"Here is my approach to this problem using . Add the following fixture to . Request this fixture from a test by specifying  as an argument to your test. When calling this fixture with a filename (e.g.  it will generate a folder structure of the form  and create the resource file if it doesn&#39;t exist yet (convenient for finding where the file should go). Finally, the call will return the path to the file. The advantage of this solution is that you don&#39;t need to do any path munging in your tests.\nFixture:\n\nExample test:\n\n","excerpt":"__name__)\n\n    def resolve_and_create_if_missing(filename):\n        <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> = os.path.join(test_resource_dir, filename)\n        if not os.path.exists(<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>):\n            # creating the dir &hellip; os.makedirs(test_resource_dir, exist_ok=True)\n            # creating the file\n            open(<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>, &quot;a&quot;).close()\n        return <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>\n\n    return resolve_and_create_if_missing\n\nExample test &hellip; ","title":"How can I get files within the tests in Python?"},{"tags":["python","csv"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":72428896,"is_answered":false,"question_id":72428797,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1653881709,"creation_date":1653881709,"body":"The r should me outside the quotes.\nAnd if u want to join path try using &quot;os.path.join(folderPath, fileName)&quot;\nExample\n\n","excerpt":"And if u want to join path try using &quot;os.path.join(folderPath, fileName)&quot;\nExample\nimport os \n\nfolder_path = r&quot;C:\\Users\\hh\\OneDrive - Technology, Inc\\IE&quot;\nfile_name = &quot;file.csv&quot;\n\n<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> = os.path.join &hellip; ","title":"Invalid argument in Python"},{"tags":["c++","qt","qt-creator","qlabel","qtwidgets"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":0,"is_answered":false,"question_id":71928090,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1650384803,"creation_date":1650384803,"body":"I&#39;m currently working on reviving an old project and can&#39;t get an image to display on the QLabel as it used to. This is a small trip advisory app that uses Dijkstra&#39;s Algorithm to calculate the shortest/most cost-efficient path from a baseball stadium to another, an image should display on the bottom right of the screen with a line drawn on top of the images from stadium to stadium and the total miles when the display trip button is pressed, however, the image is not showing.\n\nHere is my code for maindown.cpp:\n\nAny help is highly appreciated.\n","excerpt":"pen and pen color\n    QPen red ((QColor(255,0,0)),1);\n\n    painter.setPen(red);\n\n    //From here down it creates the shortest path to the current\n\n\n    int final_distance = 0;\n\n    vector&lt;string&gt; <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; npos)\n            {\n                final_index = i;\n            }\n        }\n\n\n\n\n        Dijkstra_MLB d(MLB_graph, initial_index, MLB_stadiums, final_index);\n\n\n\n        final_distance = d.dijkstra(<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; ","title":"Qt Creator - C++ - QLabel not displaying image on mainwindow"},{"tags":["python","conda","snakemake","virtual-environment"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":71825092,"item_type":"question","score":1,"last_activity_date":1649677654,"creation_date":1649667367,"body":"I have a rule in my snakemake pipeline to run multiqc :\n\nwith the conda env file multiqc.yaml :\n\nWhen I run the pipeline I&#39;ve got the following error :\n\nI&#39;ve tested to activate the conda environment manually and indeed there is the same error when running multiqc.\nI&#39;ve tested to create a conda env thanks to &quot;multiqc.yaml&quot; outside snakemake, and in this case multiqc is running correctly.\nIf someone has an idea about this it would help me a lot!\nThank you in advance\n","excerpt":"I have a rule in my snakemake pipeline to run multiqc :\n    rule summaryReport:\n        input:\n            fastqc_forward = expand(<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> + &quot;/fastqc/{sample}_R1_fastqc.html&quot;, sample = samples), &hellip; fastqc_rev = expand(<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> + &quot;/fastqc/{sample}_R2_fastqc.html&quot;, sample = samples)\n        output:\n            report = <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> + &quot;/fastqc/report_quality_control.html&quot;\n        params: &hellip; ","title":"Snakemake with integrated conda env not properly installed"},{"tags":["c++","qt","batch-file","winapi"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":71311512,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1646152948,"creation_date":1646151352,"body":"I have a directory in the current working path of my executable which is called Store. In this directory, there is a bat file which is called init.bat. I have written the following code to run this file, but it seems CreateProcessW doesn&#39;t run the bat file. How should I fix this code? I didn&#39;t receive any error, the program just doesn&#39;t work and my bat file doesn&#39;t execute.\n\n","excerpt":"ZeroMemory(&amp;pi, sizeof(pi));\n\n    QString path = QDir::toNativeSeparators(qApp-&gt;applicationDirPath());\n    path.append(L&quot;\\\\Store\\\\init.bat&quot;);\n\n    // Execute requirement batch file\n    LPWSTR <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; = _wcsdup(path.toStdWString().c_str());\n\n    status = CreateProcessW(NULL, <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>, NULL, NULL, FALSE, CREATE_NO_WINDOW, NULL, NULL, &amp;si, &amp;pi);\n\n    if(status &amp;&amp; WaitForSingleObject(pi.hProcess, INFINITE &hellip; ","title":"Execute a bat file with parent process privilege in qt"},{"tags":["python","recursion","combinations","distinct"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":71284757,"is_answered":false,"question_id":71284575,"item_type":"answer","score":2,"last_activity_date":1645981960,"creation_date":1645964918,"body":"Your original function was generating properly the possible combinations. The only problem was that you were printing it, instead of saving it in a list to use later.\nI modified it so it saves the result into a list, that can be input to a function that generates all permutations (also implemented recursively) of a list.\nOverall, the approach is:\n\nGenerate all combinations of 1, 2, ... n numbers that sum to less than \nGenerate all the permutations of these combinations (this step seems useless, because sum is commutative, but I&#39;m assuming that is the definition of your problem)\n\nNote that this is an instance of the Subset Sum Problem, which is a difficult optimisation problem. The solution below does not scale well for large sets.\n\n","excerpt":"next_path.append(current_element)\n            unique_combination(in_list[idx+1:], goal, next_sum, next_path, accumulator)\n        elif next_sum == goal:  # Arrived at a solution\n            <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; = list(current_path)\n            final_path.append(current_element)\n            accumulator.append(<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>)\n        else:   # Since in_list is ordered, all other numbers will fail after this &hellip; ","title":"Find Distinct parts of List that Sum are equal to the given Number"},{"tags":["python","python-3.x","windows"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":71092843,"is_answered":false,"question_id":71089237,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1644676872,"creation_date":1644676872,"body":"Here is the solution for this:-\n\n","excerpt":"else:\n            break\n    return selected_folder\n\nfile_path = &quot;Folder_path&quot;\nfolder_path = os.path.join(current_dir, file_path)\n\nif os.path.isdir(folder_path):\n    <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> = folder_path\nelse:\n    print &hellip; <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> = takefolder()\n\ntry:    \n    SetFileAttributes(<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>, FILE_ATTRIBUTE_HIDDEN)\n    print(&quot;The folder is now hidden!&quot;)\nexcept:\n    print(&quot;No directory found.&quot;) &hellip; ","title":"Windows : How to hide a folder with a specific path in Python"},{"tags":["rust","atomic"],"question_score":5,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":70936083,"is_answered":false,"question_id":70362352,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1643694649,"creation_date":1643694649,"body":"The  crate is a convenient option.  has a  method that performs the atomic rename.\n\nThis just does the same thing as your solution, although it is also implemented to work on Windows.\nThe temp file and final file must be on the same filesystem, which is why  explicitly specifies the temp directory.\n","excerpt":"NamedTempFile has a persist method that performs the atomic rename.\nlet <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> = std::path::Path::new(&quot;some/dir/file&quot;);\nlet mut file = tempfile::NamedTempFile::new_in(final_path.parent().unwrap())? &hellip; ;\nfile.persist(<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>)?;\n\nThis just does the same thing as your solution, although it is also implemented to work on Windows. &hellip; ","title":"Atomic file create &amp; write"},{"tags":["vue.js","julia","quasar","stipple","genie.jl"],"question_score":5,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":70672551,"item_type":"question","score":5,"last_activity_date":1642036921,"creation_date":1641930301,"body":"I have been developing an application in Julia using Genie Framework and Stipple, and the main task of this app is to implement Sobel and Prewitt operator. The problem that I am struggling with is the uploader component. So basically I am able to upload an image, on button click the image is transformed, but then when i upload another image and try to output the transformed version of it, the output that i get is still the old image. I have been trying to find the issue and I noticed that QUploader API has some methods that could help solve this problem:  method, or  method, but I do not know how to call/use these functions regarding Julia syntax. Are there any solutions available?\n\n","excerpt":"const FILE_PATH = &quot;public/sample.jpg&quot;\nconst <span class=\"highlight\">FINAL_PATH</span> = &quot;final.jpg&quot;\n#const IMGPATH = &quot;demo.png&quot;\n\nmodel = Model |&gt; init\n\n\non(model.process_s3) do _\n    model.imageurl[] = &quot;&quot;\n    @info &quot;Working&quot;\n\n    img &hellip; (sobel(sobel_image, sobel3_kernel_x, sobel3_kernel_y))\n    save(joinpath(@__DIR__, &quot;public&quot;, <span class=\"highlight\">FINAL_PATH</span>), lastImage)\n    model.imageurl[] = &quot;/$<span class=\"highlight\">FINAL_PATH</span>#$(Base.time())&quot; * string(rand())\n\n    @info model.imageurl &hellip; ","title":"QUploader component in Julia"},{"tags":["vue.js","julia","quasar","stipple","genie.jl"],"question_score":5,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":70690355,"is_answered":false,"question_id":70672551,"item_type":"answer","score":3,"last_activity_date":1642036921,"creation_date":1642036921,"body":"Replace:\n\nwith\n\nExplanation:\n makes just an anchor link to an HTML document. This will obviously result in buffering the document as the browser might just look for different anchors (and not find them) yet has no motivation to re-download.\nOn the other hand adding the  makes the request actually different every time (understood by browser as a different document). In result the cache will not be used - a new copy gets requested.\n","excerpt":"Replace:\n&quot;/$<span class=\"highlight\">FINAL_PATH</span>#$(Base.time())&quot;\n\nwith\n&quot;/$(<span class=\"highlight\">FINAL_PATH</span>)?t=$(Base.time())&quot;\n\nExplanation:\n# makes just an anchor link to an HTML document. &hellip; ","title":"QUploader component in Julia"},{"tags":["python","networkx"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":70418640,"is_answered":false,"question_id":70397567,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1639985507,"creation_date":1639985507,"body":"Not sure if this approach would work, but one option is to find the shortest path from the initial 37 nodes and then select the shortest one. Something like (pseudocode only):\n\nOne final thing is that instead of removing paths, you could create a copy of the  graph (or whatever is the prior network) and then add the final path to it using  docs.\n","excerpt":"short_paths[0]\n\nOne final thing is that instead of removing paths, you could create a copy of the Z1 graph (or whatever is the prior network) and then add the final path to it using nx.add_path(G, <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; ","title":"Removing all edges to a node except the shortest one"}],"has_more":true,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":220}